{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Toney homes have been standing for decades — some for generations. That's a lot of years for water to damage fascia boards, especially when gutters leak or overflow. Add Alabama's humidity, and you've got perfect conditions for wood rot.</p>

<p>We see it constantly on older Toney properties: gutters that seem fine but are attached to fascia that's soft, water-stained, or visibly rotting. Sometimes the damage is obvious; sometimes it's hidden under paint. Either way, it needs repair before new gutters make sense.</p>

<p>Installing new gutters on damaged fascia is a waste of money. The gutters won't stay attached properly, and the underlying rot will continue spreading. Fix the foundation first, then protect it with quality gutters.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start with thorough inspection, checking fascia (and soffit if needed) for water damage, soft spots, and rot. We'll show you exactly what we find and explain what needs repair.</p>

<p>For damaged sections, we carefully remove existing gutters, cut out rotted wood, and install new fascia boards. We use quality primed lumber or composite materials appropriate for your home's age and style.</p>

<p>Then we install new gutters on the fresh fascia — properly attached, properly sloped, and designed to prevent the overflow that caused the original damage.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia on Toney homes for over 20 years. We understand older construction, know how to match materials to historic homes, and appreciate the craftsmanship that went into properties built generations ago.</p>

<p>Our crews handle both carpentry and gutter installation. That means one company, one visit (usually), and one warranty covering everything from your fascia boards to your downspout extensions.</p>
{% endset %}
